<article class="post">
  <header class="post__header">
    <h1 class="post__title">Searching Star Trek: from keyword search to agentic AI</h1>
    <p class="post__dek">
      Three different ways of building a search tool with rankings: traditional keyword queries using a dictionary,
      RAG (an embedding-assisted answer), and agentic AI.
    </p>
  </header>

  <p>
    This tool uses real Star Trek episode scripts to demonstrate common implementations of document search:
    standard keyword search; an embedding-driven semantic search with an AI summary
    (called Retrieval-Augmented Generation, or RAG); and finally, an agentic AI with the ability to query the
    database itself.
  </p>


  <nav class="post__toc" aria-label="In this post">
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#level-1">Level 1: Keyword search with a dictionary</a></li>
      <li><a href="#level-2">Level 2: RAG with an AI summary</a></li>
      <li><a href="#level-3">Level 3: An agent with a SQL tool</a></li>
      <li><a href="#takeaways">So which one should you use?</a></li>
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <section class="post__section" id="level-1" aria-labelledby="level-1-title">
    <p class="post__eyebrow">Level 1</p>
    <h2 id="level-1-title" class="level__title">Keyword search with a dictionary</h2>
    <div class="demo">
      <%= render "dialogues/demo", home: true %>
    </div>

    <h3 class="how-it-works">How it works</h3>
    <p>
      Postgres has support for Google-like search syntax built in through <code>websearch_to_tsquery</code>. It
      requires adding a column to your database which compares individual words against a dictionary to standardize
      them. It allows you to use AND and OR operators in your search. If you're willing to do a little more
      configuration, it's also possible to correct common query misspellings on the fly.
    </p>
<pre class="code"><code>SELECT *, ts_rank_cd(searchable_english, q) AS rank
FROM dialogues, websearch_to_tsquery('english', 'assimilating') q
WHERE searchable_english @@ q
ORDER BY rank DESC;</code></pre>
    <p>
      <code>websearch_to_tsquery</code> checks your document using Google-style syntax: <code>"exact phrases"</code>,
      <code>OR</code> and <code>-exclusions</code>. When you add the English dictionary config, Postgres reduces
      words to their stems, so “assimilating” matches “assimilate”, “assimilated” and “assimilation”, as well as
      some common misspellings.
    </p>

    <figure class="post__figure">
      <%= render "home/keyword_example" %>
      <figcaption>Exact-word search vs. the English dictionary.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      <strong>Downsides:</strong> you have to know the phrase used in the actual text.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="post__section" id="level-2" aria-labelledby="level-2-title">
    <p class="post__eyebrow">Level 2</p>
    <h2 id="level-2-title" class="level__title">RAG: retrieval with an AI summary</h2>
    <div class="demo">
      <%= render "episode_texts/demo", home: true %>
    </div>

    <h3 class="how-it-works">How it works</h3>
    <p>
      Searching by meaning starts with embeddings, numerical representations of what a term “means”. Or more
      precisely, its distance from other words in 1,536 dimensions (not labeled or known dimensions, but
      distinctions the model learned to make in its training). Semantic search involves comparing the query
      embedding with the embedding of a short passage from the text, and seeing how similar the topic or “meaning”
      of that text is. The embedding for “swimming” would be more similar to “beach” than to “dessert”, for
      example. The model has learned this by association: which words commonly pair together.
    </p>
    <p>
      To create the document embeddings in the example below, I split each transcript into scenes, cutting wherever
      the script names a new location (<code>[Bridge]</code>, <code>[Ten Forward]</code>), generated an embedding for
      each scene, and stored them in a vector database. That's
      <%= number_with_delimiter(@stats[:chunks]) %> scenes so far (<%= @indexed_series.to_sentence.presence || "none yet" %>).
      A search embeds the query and asks for its nearest neighbors.
    </p>
    <p class="footnote">
      My first version cut every 2,000 characters, overlapping each chunk with the last so a conversation split
      at a boundary would still appear whole in one of them. That's the usual default in RAG tutorials, but it
      ignores the script's own structure. A scene is the unit people actually describe (“the scene where…”):
      one place, one set of characters, one beat of plot. Very short scenes are merged into the next one, and
      the few over 3,000 characters are split on line breaks.
    </p>
<pre class="code"><code>SELECT episode_text_id, content, embedding &lt;=&gt; $1 AS distance
FROM episode_chunks
ORDER BY distance
LIMIT 40;</code></pre>

    <figure class="post__figure">
      <%= render "home/rag_example" %>
      <figcaption>The top scenes, with their similarity scores, and a summary that cites them.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      <strong>Where it begins to fall short:</strong> semantic search may be good at finding relevant snippets, but
      it returns just that: snippets of text relevant to a single query. Questions that touch multiple topics might
      not be answerable with a single query, and when it comes to counting or answering numerical questions, text
      snippets are completely the wrong tool. Which brings us to our next “level-up” in sophistication…
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="post__section" id="level-3" aria-labelledby="level-3-title">
    <p class="post__eyebrow">Level 3</p>
    <h2 id="level-3-title" class="level__title">An AI agent with a SQL tool</h2>
    <div class="demo">
      <%= render "questions/demo", home: true %>
    </div>

    <h3 class="how-it-works">How it works</h3>
    <p>
      For the last
      level I gave the model two tools it can run: <code>run_sql</code>, and <code>search_scenes</code>, which is
      the vector search from level 2. The two tools work together: “how many lines does Worf have in the episode where his son
      arrives?” is a scene search to find the episode, then a <code>COUNT</code> in that episode.
    </p>
<pre class="code"><code>loop do
  output = openai.responses(input:, tools: [run_sql, search_scenes])
  break answer(output) unless output.tool_call?
  input &lt;&lt; run_tool(output).to_json   # rows, matching episodes, or the error to fix
end</code></pre>
    <p>
      Letting a language model run SQL against your database warrants some security precautions. Here are some
      ways to prevent AI agents from tampering with your data:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>run the AI's query as a subquery inside a <code>SELECT</code>, so it can only be a single read;</li>
      <li>send at most 50 rows back;</li>
      <li>set a 4-second timeout, in a read-only transaction that is always rolled back;</li>
      <li>most importantly, give the agent a separate database login: a Postgres role that can <code>SELECT</code>
        the <code>dialogues</code> and <code>episode_texts</code> tables and nothing else. Postgres itself then
        refuses writes, other tables and file-reading functions, however the SQL is written.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="footnote">
      My first version switched roles on the app's own connection (<code>SET ROLE</code>) and backed it up with a
      blocklist of dangerous functions and an <code>EXPLAIN</code> check of which tables a query touched. The role
      switch turned out not to be a boundary: the app logs in as a superuser, and a query can switch itself back
      with <code>set_config('role', 'none', true)</code>. With its own login there's nothing to switch back to, so
      the blocklist and the <code>EXPLAIN</code> check went. (This hosted demo runs on a database plan that can't
      add logins, so here the agent shares the app's login and the read-only transaction is what stops writes.)
    </p>

    <figure class="post__figure">
      <%= render "home/agent_example" %>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="post__section" id="takeaways" aria-labelledby="takeaways-title">
    <h2 id="takeaways-title">So which one should you use?</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="post__table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>Keyword</th><th>RAG</th><th>Agent</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th>You provide</th><td>the exact words</td><td>a description</td><td>a question</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Typical time</th><td>well under 1 s</td><td>~2 s, then a streamed answer</td><td>3–8 s</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Cost per search</th><td>free</td><td>two model calls</td><td>a few model calls</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Good at</th><td>quotes, catchphrases</td><td>“the one where…”</td><td>counting, ranking, comparing</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Fails by</th><td>finding nothing</td><td>summarizing the wrong scenes</td><td>being confidently wrong about bad data</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      My personal take, although I'm not a data scientist: if you have some background knowledge about what
      keywords to search for, then anecdotally, I can't say that a “zero config” search of embeddings is better at
      returning what a user would consider “accurate” results. Its strength is its ability to cast a wide net and
      pull in documents on a relevant topic, and with the wide context windows of today's LLMs, it's usually more
      than enough context.
    </p>
  </section>
</article>
